/P 329 0 R
/T (The data presents significant variation across countries. Singapore's Information and Communication sector shows approximately 40% female participation as of 2024 \(based on the 2024 BCG publication "Closing the Gender Gap in Technology in Southeast Asia"\), indicating a persistent gender gap despite the country's advanced digital infrastructure. Malaysia exhibits stronger female representation, with women comprising 66.52 percent of the ICT workforce in 2022 and 67.92 percent in 2023, reaching a peak of 69.67 percent in 2021. These figures suggest a relatively strong track record in engaging women in tech-related employment; however, additional disaggregation would be necessary to evaluate participation in specific roles or levels of seniority. )
/E (The data presents significant variation across countries. Singapore's Information and Communication sector shows approximately 40% female participation as of 2024 \(based on the 2024 BCG publication "Closing the Gender Gap in Technology in Southeast Asia"\), indicating a persistent gender gap despite the country's advanced digital infrastructure. Malaysia exhibits stronger female representation, with women comprising 66.52 percent of the ICT workforce in 2022 and 67.92 percent in 2023, reaching a peak of 69.67 percent in 2021. These figures suggest a relatively strong track record in engaging women in tech-related employment; however, additional disaggregation would be necessary to evaluate participation in specific roles or levels of seniority. )
